From 79d975748fb180e3c6d38d7a5207415b28fc98b0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arena Agent <agent@arena.ai>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 12:57:38 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 15/29] Document what the probe got wrong, and what the guard
 did on the phone

The progress log claimed a bug in the probe "can never lock someone out of
their own phone". Four launches on the device later, it had - three refusals
from an instance-level entry point asked for with no instance, and the skip
path it took there is now deliberately a reason not to launch. Written up in
docs/gpu-safety.md with both bugs, the fake-loader harness that now catches
them in CI before gradle runs, and the one case the harness itself got wrong
by assuming a machine with no libvulkan.

The field result is the reason any of this exists: four refusals, no reboot,
the uptime running 10h42m across them.

Co-authored-by: arena-agent <297053741+arena-agent@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 docs/gpu-safety.md   | 39 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++----
 docs/progress-log.md | 17 ++++++++++++++---
 2 files changed, 49 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

diff --git a/docs/gpu-safety.md b/docs/gpu-safety.md
index 3fed494..3c929f2 100644
--- a/docs/gpu-safety.md
+++ b/docs/gpu-safety.md
@@ -88,15 +88,46 @@ built. Three stages, each with its own clock:
 3. **Readback** — the bytes come back as they were written. A fence that is signalled without the
    work having been done cannot pass.
 
-Only a fence that never signals counts as a hard failure. Anything that prevents the test being
-built — no `python3`, no suitable queue family, no host-visible memory, a struct the ICD refuses —
-is a **skip**, never a verdict against the GPU. A bug in this script must never be the thing that
-locks someone out of their own phone.
+Only a fence that never signals counts as a hard verdict **against the GPU**. Anything that
+prevents the test being built — no `python3`, no suitable queue family, no host-visible memory, a
+struct the ICD refuses — is reported as a **skip**, which says "the GPU was not tested".
+
+Since 2026-10-10 the app treats a skip as a reason **not to launch** (it is labelled
+`DD_GPU_PROBE_SKIPPED` in the log and the panel), which is a change from the original intent, and
+it was made after the skip path was observed on a real device doing exactly what a skip cannot
+distinguish: an ICD that refused the probe and an ICD that would have hung the phone look the same
+from here. The cost is honest and it has been paid — a bug in the probe now locks the session out
+(see below), and it is unlocked from the Security panel, not by bricking the device. The guard
+errs that way on purpose: the reversible error is the cheap one.
 
 The app owns the outer clock (60 s) as well as the guest's internal ones, and if the process is
 still alive it asks proot to wind its tree down (SIGTERM, then SIGKILL) before recording the
 timeout as an answer.
 
+#### Two bugs the probe shipped with, and the test that now runs them
+
+Worth writing down because both were invisible in exactly the way that matters.
+
+1. It asked `vkGetInstanceProcAddr(NULL, "vkDestroyInstance")` and took the NULL as news about the
+   driver. A loader only has to answer global commands with no instance, and `vkDestroyInstance`
+   is instance-level: it was the probe asking the wrong question, and the answer was correct. The
+   result was a skip on every launch — which, since the paragraph above, blocks the launch. Three
+   attempts on a real phone, all stopped by a bug in the test, none by the GPU.
+2. `VkPhysicalDeviceProperties` was declared without its leading `sType` and `pNext`, so every
+   field was read 16 bytes low. The "device name" was the driver version. The check meant to prove
+   the right ICD had answered was reading the wrong bytes and saying nothing was wrong.
+
+Neither would have survived being run once, so it is run: `tools/test/mock_vulkan.c` is a fake
+Vulkan loader (gcc, python3, nothing destined for the phone) that answers NULL for instance
+commands until an instance exists, reports a Mali-G615 MC6, performs the fill for real, and can be
+told what the fence says. `tools/test/run_probe_tests.sh` drives it through six cases and the
+build workflow runs it **before** gradle, so a broken probe fails the build instead of the phone.
+
+That harness also caught a third thing, about itself: the case "no loader can be opened" passed
+here and failed on `ubuntu-24.04`, because the runner ships a system `libvulkan.so.1`. A test that
+asserts what only some machines do is worse than no test. The loader is now selectable by name
+(`DD_GPU_PROBE_LOADER`), which makes the case deterministic everywhere.
+
 ### 4. Takes the session down first — `GpuWatchdog`
 
 Two samples a second, while a session runs:
diff --git a/docs/progress-log.md b/docs/progress-log.md
index ecbdde8..140dbc6 100644
--- a/docs/progress-log.md
+++ b/docs/progress-log.md
@@ -29,15 +29,26 @@ So the guard is built around the four things an APK *can* do. Full write-up: `do
   upscaler or effects, no second screen, and the client's interface on llvmpipe - which doubles as
   a diagnosis: stable on Strict but not on Safe means the fault is in the Vulkan path.
 - **Test before Steam does** (`Probe`, `droiddeck-gpu-probe`). Enumeration under `timeout`, then a
-  ctypes `vkCmdFillBuffer` + fence wait against the session's ICD, then a readback. Only a fence
-  that never signals is a failure; anything that stops the test being built is a **skip**, so a bug
-  in the probe can never lock someone out of their own phone.
+  ctypes `vkCmdFillBuffer` + fence wait against the session's ICD, then a readback. A fence that
+  never signals is a failure against the GPU; anything that stops the test being built is a **skip**
+  - "the GPU was not tested", which the app now treats as a reason not to launch, because untested
+  and about-to-hang are indistinguishable from here and only one of them is reversible.
 - **Stop the session first** (`GpuWatchdog`). Frames (`recentFrameIntervalMs`), processes wedged in
   `D` state (3+ of our own uid, 3 samples running, ~1.5 s - inside the kernel's 3 s fence timeout),
   and temperature. An abort sweeps the tree four seconds later.
 - **Leave evidence.** `gpu-guard.log` and `gpu-probe.log` per session, a GPU-safety section in
   `device.txt`, and a fifth Setup tab (**Safety**, Mali only) with a Copy button for bug reports.
 
+**First field result (same day, on the device).** Four launches, four refusals, **no reboot** - the
+uptime in the log runs 10h42m across the attempts, so the phone stayed up all night with the guard
+holding the door. Three of the four refusals were the guard's own bug, not the GPU's: the probe
+asked the loader for an instance-level entry point with no instance and read the correct NULL as a
+fault. Both that bug and a second one (a `VkPhysicalDeviceProperties` missing its first two
+fields, which made every device property read 16 bytes low) are now caught by a probe test suite
+that runs against a fake Vulkan loader in CI, before the app is built. The panel also shows what
+`sys.boot.reason` looks like on this device: "not readable" - it is refused here, so the guard's
+own session record is the mechanism that matters, and it worked. See `docs/gpu-safety.md`.
+
 Deliberately not done, and documented as such in `docs/gpu-safety.md`: nothing touches the kernel
 module, SELinux, a watchdog, or the fence timeout - none of that is an app's to change, and the
 probe can itself provoke the fault it is looking for. The guard makes the panic harder to repeat
